  </div>

  <!-- Deep-Dive Science Explanatory Guide Section -->
  <section class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-3xl p-6 md:p-10 space-y-6">
    <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-4">
      <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-700 block mb-1">
        Clinical Microbiology &amp; Ceramic Glaze Metallurgy
      </span>
      <h3 class="text-title-lg font-medium text-ink tracking-tight">
        {d.guideTitle}
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-muted">{d.guideSub}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-xs md:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
      <div class="space-y-3 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-amber-600 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          FDA CPG Sec 545.450 &amp; Acid-Catalyzed Lead Extraction
        </h4>
        <p>
          Lead oxide (PbO) was historically added to earthenware ceramic glazes as a fluxing agent, reducing melting temperatures and imparting a glossy, defect-free vitreous sheen. When acidic solutions like tomatoes (pH 4.0), vinegar (pH 3.2), or wine contact the glaze, hydrogen ions actively exchange with divalent lead (Pb²⁺) and cadmium (Cd²⁺) ions within the silicate network. Under continuous 170°F–200°F (77°C–93°C) thermal exposure over 8 to 12 hours, heavy metal extraction increases exponentially compared to room temperature laboratory bench tests.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-rose-600 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          The Red Kidney Bean Phytohaemagglutinin (PHA) Hazard
        </h4>
        <p>
          Red kidney beans (<em>Phaseolus vulgaris</em>) contain exceptionally high concentrations of phytohaemagglutinin, a cytotoxic lectin that binds to and damages small intestinal microvilli. While vigorous boiling at 212°F (100°C) for 10 minutes completely denatures the lectin (lowering toxicity below 400 HAU), heating at sub-boiling temperatures of 176°F (80°C) activates and concentrates the lectin, increasing toxicity up to 5-fold (100,000+ HAU). Canned beans are already pressure-cooked and safe, but raw dried kidney beans must never be slow-cooked without a 10-minute stovetop boil.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-amber-600 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          Clostridium perfringens &amp; The 4-Hour Danger Zone Rule
        </h4>
        <p>
          <em>Clostridium perfringens</em> is one of the most common bacterial causes of food poisoning. It produces hardy heat-resistant endospores that survive initial cooking heat. Between 109°F and 125°F (43°C–52°C), these spores germinate into vegetative cells with doubling times as rapid as 10 to 12 minutes. Placing large frozen roasts directly into a slow cooker extends danger-zone dwell times beyond 4 to 6 hours, allowing spore outgrowth and enterotoxin formation before lethal temperatures are reached.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          Glaze Crazing &amp; Subsurface Earthenware Porosity
        </h4>
        <p>
          Repetitive heating and cooling cycles create differential thermal expansion between the ceramic glaze and underlying earthenware clay body, creating microscopic networks of hairline spider-web fractures known as crazing. Once crazed, hot acidic braising liquids bypass the surface glaze barrier and permeate deep into the porous ceramic substrate, trapping bacteria and dissolving trace mineral impurities directly into future meals.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

